Please write a concurrent program in Java 8 that implements the following functionality: Create a Java class called DelegatingRobotTracker that monitors the coordinates of robots in a thread-safe manner using a ConcurrentHashMap. The class should provide methods to check the coordinates of a specific robot, modify the coordinates of a robot, and return an unmodifiable view of all robot coordinates. Use delegation to ensure thread safety without explicitly synchronizing methods. Initialize the tracker with a map of robot IDs to their initial coordinates. Include two versions of the method to retrieve all coordinates: one that returns a live unmodifiable map backed by the original data, and another that returns a static snapshot of the current state as an unmodifiable map. Ensure that modifying a robot's coordinates only succeeds if the robot ID already exists in the map. The requirements are as follows:

All classes and methods must be defined within a single .java file;
The program must include a public static void main(String[] args) method as the entry point;
No external libraries or dependencies should be used;
The code should be clear, well-structured, and follow Java 8 syntax standards;
If multiple classes are needed, they should all be placed in the same file, with non-public classes defined using the class keyword.
The implementation must use multithreading to perform tasks concurrently. You may use Thread, Runnable, or ExecutorService from the standard Java library to achieve this. Ensure that thread safety is considered where necessary.
The total number of threads used in the program, including the main thread, must not exceed 3. Use a fixed thread pool or manually manage at most 2 additional threads beyond the main thread. Avoid unbounded thread creation or cached thread pools.
Do not add any annotations.

